RL agents can utilize patterns in exogenous components of rewards to accelerate learning, as seen when a robot vacuuming learns from house occupancy signals. This work formulates a formal problem to address the identification of exogenous reward components and the resulting speedup in RL. We establish a variance-covariance-based condition that guarantees an reduction in variance when estimating the value function of an endogenous MDP using Monte Carlo policy evaluation, as opposed to the full MDP with exogenous states. We propose two methods for decomposing rewards into exogenous and endogenous parts and evaluate them on multiple tasks. Our findings show that this decomposition can significantly speed up RL algorithms, supporting the use of exogenous states in practice.
