RL agents can improve their learning by leveraging patterns in exogenous rewards, such as when a robot vacuuming infers information from the presence of dirt. This paper formulates the problem of identifying exogenous reward components and the subsequent acceleration of reinforcement learning. We derive a variance-covariance-based condition that ensures a reduction in valuation error when estimating the value of an endogenous MDP via Monte Carlo policy evaluation, compared to using the entire MDP. We propose two algorithms for this decomposition and demonstrate that they can substantially speed up RL, validating the practical application of exogenous states.

